Linda Lansford is with Scout Troop 17 in Nashville, Tennessee,

20
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
And she said: thank you so much for posting the video about autism and scouting and one boy's personal journey.

21
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And if you didn't see that, that was a video that I posted last week that I found on YouTube,

22
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
just kind of one of those serendipitous things that every once in a while you run across,

23
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And it's a wonderful story about one scout's experience in scouting and how the folks that were involved supported him

24
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
and helped him through some challenges with autism And it's really, really great.

25
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
But anyway, Linda went on to say: one minute in, I was literally in tears.

26
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I identified with Chase's mother- and Chase was the name of the scout in the video-

27
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So much that her words could be mine. I'm the mother of a 12-year-old scout with Asperger's.

28
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
When we first joined Troop 17 in Nashville it was hard for me to step back

29
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
and let my son fully experience scouting. I'm so used to managing his world for him.

30
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,000
The Troop leader took the time to reassure me, telling me that my son was not the first autistic scout he'd worked with

31
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
and that everything would be okay. And it's so much more than okay. It's amazing.

32
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The Troop is teaching him to be self-sufficient and teaching me to have faith, trust and let go.

33
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
We're starting our third year with Troop 17, and I have a very different child.

34
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Each time he goes away to summer or winter camp he comes home with a little more confident,

35
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
a little more mature, a little farther down the road to becoming an amazing young man.

36
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Thank you so much, Linda, for getting in touch,

37
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:59,000
And that is a story that we are increasingly hearing about autistic children being able to draw a lot from their scouting experience.

38
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
It seems almost as though that scouting was designed to address some of the challenges that they come up with,

39
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
and it's just a wonderful thing to hear that it does so.

40
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
And I know- I know from personal experience- it's very, very challenging the first time you go through this

41
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,000
as a scouter and you learn a lot. but, man, what an enriching experience for you as a scouter,

42
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
as well as for the scout and their family too.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
So thank you once again, Linda, for being in touch.

44
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
I want to tell you that I was very honored to spend this past weekend in the company

45
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
and a number of scouts from the Baden Powell Service Association, the BPSA.

46
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
What wonderful folks. I've got a lot of material to cover in this podcast,

47
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
so I'll save that whole story for another time perhaps.

48
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I was very heartened to meet these wonderful people in the BPSA

49
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and I got to become a rover scout- How about that?

50
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Yes, R-O-V-E-R rover,

51
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And, like I said, we have a lot to do today, so I'll have to save that story for another time,

52
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
but it's a pretty good one.

53
