and I'll make sure to thank you during our next podcast.

82
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Well, the remainder of this podcast is catching up on email questions and answers

83
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
and that will indeed stretch out for a while and take up the rest of this podcast.

84
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So let's get started. shall we

85
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Find me a letter? send it my name,

86
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Email. that is, folks. And here's an answer to one of your emails.

87
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:10,000
So the first email- I'm going to hold all of the identifying characteristics of it in confidence,

88
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
But this came from the scoutmaster of a newer troop and he said:

89
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
we are having a discussion about the cooking requirement for tenderfoot

90
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
and we're wondering exactly how we should judge that.

91
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Does helping get lunch ready by, you know, just kind of helping them around with the condiments

92
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and getting the bread out and stuff like that to make sandwiches, Is that cooking?

93
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
And if a scout is just kind of assisting with another person with the cooking,

94
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
is that really cooking And we're having a tough time deciding exactly

95
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
how a scout's fulfilled that requirement.

96
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Well, I think it's important- not just on this particular tenderfoot requirement, right,

97
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
But for any of the requirements that we use- is to step back a little bit

98
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and read the requirement carefully and you know what.

99
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Once you do it becomes pretty clear and you don't have to have a big discussion about how to do it.

100
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
It's right there.

101
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
This particular tenderfoot requirement says, quote:

102
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
on the camp out: assist in preparing and cooking one of your patrol's meals.

103
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Pretty simple sentence, right.

104
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,000
And if you read that and then you go off and you start having a big broad discussion about it,

105
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:23,000
you can get pretty far afield and kind of talk yourself into a corner

106
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
where you can't find an answer.

107
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
But if you just look at the requirement and kind of break it down, it's pretty simple.

108
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
So the first three words in the requirement are on the camp out, right.

109
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
So that means on a camp out.

110
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,000
It's very, very simple, not at home or anywhere else.

111
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
So we've got that right:

112
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Assist, preparing and cooking.

113
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So on the camp out, assist preparing and cooking.

114
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Preparing and cooking are obviously two different things,

115
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Otherwise we wouldn't need two words there.

116
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,000
So on the camp out, assist preparing and cooking one of your patrol's meals?

117
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Okay, so it's not just any meal,

118
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
It's not just something for yourself,

119
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
It's one of your patrol's meals.

120
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
So that wouldn't be a meal fixed by an adult or directed by one,

121
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
It's your patrol, because you know adults have nothing to do with what happens when a patrol cooks.

122
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
So the test for that particular requirement would be to say:

123
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
number one: was it on a camp out?

124
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Yes,

125
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Did the scout assist with both preparing and cooking,

126
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Which I guess you know? that could be a point of contention with a lot of people.

127
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
but hey, you know we're not building a nuclear power plant.

128
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Okay,

129
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
We're trying to do something simple and fun with scouts.

130
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,000
And if you come across something like this in a requirement that you're having a tough time trying to define,

131
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
don't create a rule for it.

132
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Ask the scout-

133
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
So what do you think cooking is?

134
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
And have that discussion.

135
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And hey, the scout may say, you know what,

136
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
I really didn't, don't think I cooked anything,

137
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
And say, okay, well then let's wait until you've actually done that,

138
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Or they may have a very good argument for why they feel that they cooked something.

139
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,000
But ask them,

140
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Get them into the conversation,

141
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Don't make it a totally adult decision.

142
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Make it something that the scout is participating in.

143
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:08,000
And then the final question in this requirement to me is: hey, who was the meal for?

144
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Was it for your patrol?

145
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And if it wasn't, well, does it qualify or not?

146
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Probably not, right,

147
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,000
It's very, very specific about those particular points.

148
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,000
So adults are not around when patrols cook their meals, necessarily.

149
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
So the person who's going to sign this requirement off, to my mind at least,

150
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
is going to be the one who worked directly with the scout,

151
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And that would likely be the patrol leader, correct?

152
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
So I'd expect the patrol leader knows the difference between preparing and cooking

153
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and let him figure out if the scout passed the requirement.

154
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
And once again, these kinds of discussions are the ones that we want to be having with scouts.

155
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
It may not sound right to you that I am suggesting that a patrol leader can sign off a requirement,

156
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
But hey, they absolutely can.

157
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
And usually when I suggest that that's one way of doing things,

158
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
people get concerned that patrol leaders are just going to skip things over and things like that.

159
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Well, no, that doesn't necessarily follow,

160
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
especially if you are working along and mentoring patrol leaders to understand requirements

161
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and be able to sign them off effectively right.

162
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
So what I read out was just the first part of that tenderfoot requirement.

163
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,000
There's another part to it.

164
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
It says: tell why it's important for each patrol member to share in meal preparation

165
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and clean up and explain the importance of eating together.

166
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And what I want to point out about that that I feel is very important

167
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
is the active verb in that particular sentence: tell.

168
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Tell means telling, not writing,

169
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
And the patrol leader, once again, can ask the scout some questions

170
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
and sign off that part of the requirement too.

171
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Now I kind of paraphrased the email that I got

172
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
because I wanted to preserve the sender's anonymity,

173
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
but they discussed in there that the parents of the particular scout

174
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
in question were kind of getting themselves involved with this.

175
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Well, I want to tell you that mother and father have absolutely nothing at all

176
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
to do with any of this.

177
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
This is something that their scout is doing.

178
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
So they're not allowed on a soccer field when the game is on

179
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and they aren't allowed in a patrol campsite

180
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
when scouts are working together as a patrol.

181
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Parents don't make the referees call at a soccer game or keep score.

182
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
They're spectators,

183
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
And that's the same thing when scouts are camping,

184
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
You can be a spectator,

185
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
You can watch from an appropriate distance,

186
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
but you may not tell the referee or the scorekeeper.

187
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
So you know, in my little allegory here

188
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
that would be the scouter or the patrol leader-

189
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,000
How to do their job.

190
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
You can be there and you can observe,

191
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
That's fine.

192
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
So after I answered the initial email that I got,

193
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,000
I got this reply.

194
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
So the parents are saying that the way they read the requirement

195
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
is that the boy assistant preparing and assistant cooking.

196
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:44,000
they feel that the actual cooking is not required.

197
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Well, you know what.

198
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Parents are often trying to be helpful

199
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and they're trying to advocate for their child

200
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and that's fine

201
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:52,000
And I understand their concerns.

202
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
but there's a judgment call involved here

203
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
and that's a call for the scouter to make

204
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and not for parents to make.

205
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
And I would very politely tell them.

206
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,000
you know I understand your concerns

207
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
and please understand that I am thoroughly invested

208
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
in making sure that your child is treated fairly.

209
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
But in this part of the scouting process,

210
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
this is really my call and my call is going to stand.

211
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
It's not set a precedent where we're going to argue

212
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
over every single little thing.

213
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Let's just have fun and be in scouts

214
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and do these things.

215
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
And I ask you to give me the benefit of a doubt here,

216
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
And I typically advise scouts that if they acquiesce-

217
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
just because there's a lot of parental pressure-

218
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
to something that they feel is unreasonable

219
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
And the way that the parents are putting this-

220
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
cooking not being actual cooking,

221
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
you know, I think that's a good example

222
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:37,000
of being unreasonable.

223
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
If you acquiesce to something like that,

224
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,000
they're going to question and complain

225
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
every single thing. from now on,

226
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Let's make it clear from the get go

227
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Who will be doing the interpreting and deciding.

228
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
Okay,

229
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Parents' input is always important.

230
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
We always want to listen to their concerns,

231
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
But we also want to understand

232
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
the way that the process works.

233
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
I heard from Mason Turner and he said

234
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
I want to jump right in and ask:

235
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
how do I get troops to give me den chiefs

236
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
for my Cub Scout pack?

237
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
We don't have any kind of great relationship

238
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
with our neighboring troops,

239
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
So how can we initiate those relationships?

240
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Do I offer them a presentation on the new Cub program

241
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,000
or something like that?

242
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Do you have any ideas for me?

243
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Well, this is kind of frustrating, Mason.

244
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
I wish your pack was down the road from us.

245
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
I've also had a lot of challenges

246
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
from the troop side of the question

247
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
in forming relationships with Cub packs

248
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
And I feel kind of like. sometimes

249
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
we go to the dance and we ask them to dance

250
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:32,000
and they just keep turning us down.

251
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
What a sad, sad allegory that one is.

252
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
But you know what, Mason?

253
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Here's what I suggest:

254
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Get on the phone with the scout masters

255
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
in the troops around you

256
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and ask them if they're willing to help you out,

257
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
that you'd like to get some scouts to serve as den chiefs.

258
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
Put the ball in their court by saying something: like

259
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
you know, we'd really like to get better connected with the troop

260
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and we'd like to have den chiefs involved with us

261
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:55,000
on a regular basis.

262
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
And the first scout master who responds

263
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
with an actual plan of action

264
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
is probably the one that you're going to end up working with

265
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Now. I asked to visit their next troop meeting

266
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
and see what the general vibe is

267
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
and talk to a couple of scouts

268
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
And if it seems like a pretty good bunch,

269
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,000
move on forward.

270
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:14,000
My bet is that if you were to call five scout masters,

271
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
at least one is going to get back to you

272
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:17,000
with a plan of action,

273
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And the ones who are put off

274
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
or pass you off to somebody else

275
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
I wouldn't really be bothered with.

276
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
But often getting that connection

277
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
is really as simple as that.

278
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Now the next email.

279
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
I'm going to leave the sender's name anonymous

280
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
And the reason for that will become apparent

281
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:33,000
as we go through it.

282
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
It's time to move to a new troop.

283
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
We've visited our new troop

284
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and I've talked with the scout master.

285
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,000
What's the best procedure for informing

286
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
our current troop that we plan to move?

287
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Do I write a letter to the troop committee

288
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
chairman or the scout master?

289
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Is there a typical way that this is handled?

290
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
So if you've been listening or reading for a while,

291
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
you will have heard email questions

292
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
where issues arise

293
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
where there's basic disagreement

294
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
with the way things are going in one scout troop,

295
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
And I typically advise the parent

296
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
or the scouter, if things just aren't

297
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
working for them in one troop, that,

298
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
hey, it's a good idea to look at others

299
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and make a decision

300
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
And to find one that more closely resembles

301
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
what you hope to have for your child

302
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
or your experience as a scouter.

303
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
And then just change troops

304
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
And I don't think this has to be a big deal at all

305
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
And I think good starting place

306
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:25,000
is the scout himself.

307
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
Talk about making the change with them,

308
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Ask them how they want to handle it,

309
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
And they may be in a position

310
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
where they would want to do something

311
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
like go and talk to the scout master

312
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
or something like that or not.

313
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
I would allow them to define exactly

314
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
how they were going to handle the move,

315
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:42,000
within reason of course,

316
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
And I get the idea of sending a letter

317
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
or something like that.

318
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,000
But a letter also has a way of backfiring on you,

319
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
because sometimes these changes

320
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
are not totally amicable

321
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
And sometimes they're caused

322
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
by pretty sharp disagreements

323
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:57,000
or difficulties,

324
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
And so a letter or an email about it

325
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
may backfire on you,

326
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
because any letter or email

327
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
is going to get the fine tooth comb treatment,

328
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
And if it's a sharp disagreement

329
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
or a conflict of some kind,

330
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
it's very unlikely that the troop

331
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
that you're leaving is going to read that

332
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and say, ooh hey, we were wrong all along.

333
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
They're more likely to brand you as a troublemaker

334
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
and let everybody else in scouting know

335
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
exactly the way that they think of it.

336
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Now isn't that unfortunate, but it happens

337
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
In most situations that I hear about.

338
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
I would roughly suggest

339
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
shake hands and say

340
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:32,000
thank you very much,

341
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,000
We've enjoyed our time here,

342
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
but we're going to be changing troops,

343
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
So this will be our last time here

344
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
and then we'll be doing

345
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
scouting over in the other troop across town

346
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
or whatever.

347
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,000
If you're asked to explain-

348
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
well, you can explain or not-

349
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
I would say maybe don't.

350
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
I would just keep the whole thing

351
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:50,000
on as high a plane

352
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and make it as amicable as possible,

353
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
because there is

354
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
plenty of needless drama

355
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,000
in scouting

356
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
and in life in general,

357
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
and your son's interest

358
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
is the most important interest here.

359
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
So let's make the elements of this change

360
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:06,000
that you can control,

361
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,000
which is your attitude

362
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
and the way that you're going to do it,

363
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
as drama-free as possible.

364
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
Robert got in touch with me with this question.

365
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
I wanted to get your thoughts on the policy manual

366
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
I was planning on creating

367
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
that explained our troop rules and traditions

368
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
and note the origin of each

369
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
in scouting literature.

370
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
I'm a fairly new scout master

371
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
and I think having this defined

372
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
for everyone would be helpful.

373
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
What do you think? Does this seem reasonable?

374
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
I know that you don't recommend

375
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
writing policy manuals.

376
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
Well, Robert, I think writing

377
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
things out like this

378
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
may be useful for you

379
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
to kind of think your way through things,

380
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
but I would suggest that it will

381
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
only be marginally useful

382
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
for parents in the committee

383
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
and it's not really

384
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
going to be very useful for your scouts.

385
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Now, is this a reasonable idea?

386
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Well, of course it makes perfect sense, right,

387
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
That you would have this document

388
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
that kind of helps define things for everybody.

389
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
But is it really a great idea?

390
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Not to my lights,

391
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and I want to explain why.

392
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
I know how strong the desire is

393
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
to explain everything to our scouts,

394
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
but my normal advice

395
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
is to resist the temptation

396
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
to explain

397
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
and participate

398
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
in the cooperative process

399
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
where scouts

400
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
discover these things for themselves.

401
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
Few if any

402
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
of the scouts that I have worked with

403
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
read manuals,

404
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
job descriptions,

405
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
policies, procedures,

406
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
and few adults do that

407
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
either.

408
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
They usually go off of what they are told

409
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and what they are used to have happening,

410
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
And I think we could ask anyone

411
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
on the end of any customer

412
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
service line

413
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
whether or not people actually read

414
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
directions and manuals,

415
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
and we'd find out that for the most part,

416
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
no, they don't.

417
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
That's why we have customer service lines, right.

418
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
The other aspect of this is,

419
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
as soon as you create a list of rules

420
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
or procedures or policies,

421
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
you're really kind of limiting yourself

422
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
to the rules and procedures

423
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
and policies that you've written.

424
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
If you give a scout a printed copy

425
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
of his job description,

426
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
that might be helpful

427
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
to what's on that piece of paper.

428
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
So what I typically suggest

429
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
is that process that I referred to earlier,

430
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
which is explaining

431
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
things as they come along

432
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
by asking questions.

433
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
So take something really really simple:

434
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
What does an assistant patrol leader do?

435
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
So you get to a meeting

436
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and the scouts come to you

437
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and say our patrol leader isn't here.

438
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
So you ask the question:

439
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
who becomes your patrol leader

440
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
if your patrol leader is absent?

441
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
and they look at you and they say,

442
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
ooh, I don't know.

443
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
They say so, who do you think

444
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
that person ought to be?

445
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
And they are staring at you

446
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
with blank faces saying,

447
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
what do you mean?

448
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
I guess we could pick somebody.

449
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Well, do you guys have an assistant patrol leader?

450
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Oh,

451
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
yeah.

452
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
So have you checked

453
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
in the scout handbook?

454
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
who takes over when the patrol leader's not here?

455
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
No,

456
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
Maybe there would be something in there about that.

457
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
So you get the idea:

458
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
Instead of answering that question with a declarative statement

459
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
or having it instituted in a piece of literature

460
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
that you created somewhere,

461
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
well, they have a scout handbook that explains all of this-

462
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
It's already written-

463
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
and getting them into the idea of taking a look

464
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
and answering their questions themselves,

465
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
you get them in a process

466
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
that begins to self-replicate

467
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
and really leverages

468
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
that process of discovery

469
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
that you want to see happen in scouts.

470
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
So when it gets down to things like:

471
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
well, what are our rules of conduction?

472
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
We have some rules of conduct.

473
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
How will these scouts know how to behave

474
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
if we don't have rules of conduct?

475
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
Well, they have the scout oath and law.

476
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
That's their rules of conduct.

477
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
You really don't need to write anything else.

478
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
You have the scout oath and law.

479
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
It's already there.

480
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
The scout handbook and the senior patrol leader's handbook

481
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
explain everything anybody needs to know

482
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
about the mechanics

483
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
and you really don't have to rewrite those.

484
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
If you want scouts to learn

485
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
how to do much of anything or how things work,

486
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
you have to let them actually do them

487
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
in real time

488
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
and then you talk about what they did

489
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
and you can introduce all kinds of ideas

490
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
this way and you can ask all kinds of questions

491
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
And rather than

492
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
setting yourself up as the authority,

493
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
they have a broader authority to go to.

494
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
they have a handbook to go to,

495
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
they have places where they can find information

496
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
and you become their partner in discovery

497
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
rather than being the source of everything.

498
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
Now I heard back from Robert and he said:

499
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
thanks for your reply.

500
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
I'm still a little unclear.

501
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
It says specifically in the scout master handbook

502
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
that rank and age requirements to be senior

503
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
patrol leader are determined by each troop,

504
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
as is the schedule of elections.

505
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
So my troop has generally said

506
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
that the senior patrol leader

507
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
needs to be at least life rank

508
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
and the election schedule is after summer camp

509
